  "query_preview": "/Through Sunday/...\nIssued at 1243 PM MST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nWater vapor satellite imagery shows an upper level trough \ncentered over California and Nevada with a very moist southwest \nflow aloft ahead of it (atmospheric river). Within this southwest \nflow aloft there is a shortwave trough embedded over the Desert \nSouthwest. Widespread rain and mountain snow will continue across \nwestern Colorado, possibly bringing historic flooding to southwest\nColorado. For our forecast area, north central and northeast \nColorado will be rain shadowed, and any precipitation is expected \nto be light under the moist southwest flow aloft. The best chance \nfor rain and high mountain snow will be late this afternoon and \nevening as the shortwave trough races northeast across Colorado. \nThe airmass remains warm tonight while southwest flow aloft \ncontinues. The snow level stays above 10,000 feet most of the \nnight before it begins to fall towards sunrise as the upper level \ntrough nears.\u00a0\n\nThe upper level trough axis will be centered over Colorado at 12Z\nMonday. As it progresses eastward it will be over the eastern \nColorado border by 00Z Tuesday. The best lift associated with the \nupper level trough will be Monday morning and into the early \nafternoon. There is still a good amount of uncertainty regarding \nrainfall amounts along the Front Range and eastern plains. Expect \na period of low level flow (below 700mb) where winds are north to \nnortheast, this should be a favorable period for rainfall. Once \nflow turns to a downslope northwesterly direction rain should \nquickly end. Timing of this transition is where the forecast \nuncertainty lies. Ensemble means are around a quarter to a third \ninch for precipitation amounts for the Front Range and plains, but\nindividual members vary from over a half inch to less than a \ntenth inch. For the mountains, snow increases around 12Z as lift \nfrom the upper level moves in. The snow will continue as flow \nturns northwesterly, providing orographic lift. Drier air slowly \nfilters into the mountains after midnight Monday night. The snow \nlevel quickly falls to 8,000 to 8,500 feet Monday morning as \ncolder air accompanies the upper level low. As far as snow totals \ngo, expect 3 to 10 inches for the mountains, heaviest across the \nnorthern mountains. A Winter Weather Advisory has been issued for \nthis. For the northeast plains, winds increase Monday, becoming \nvery windy Monday afternoon and Monday night as a surface low \ndeepens over the Central Plains. Gusts to 55 mph will be possible \nand a few gusts above 60 mph can't be ruled out in the typical \nwindy areas over the plains.\u00a0\n\nOn Tuesday, a few light rain showers will be possible over the \nplains early in the morning. Overall, dry conditions and near \nnormal temperatures are expected for Tuesday as an upper level \nridge builds over the region. The ridging doesn't last long, as a \nquick-moving, small, closed low displaces the ridge by early \nWednesday. This low moves across Colorado Wednesday, bringing an\narea rain and mountain snow. Not a lot of moisture with this \nsystem, so precipitation amounts are on track to be light.\n\nOn Thursday and Friday a disorganized weak westerly flow aloft\nwill be over the western half\u00a0of the country. Colorado will stay\nbetween a shortwave trough passing south over the Southern Rockies\nand another one to our north that will travel across the Northern\nRockies. This will leave us with dry conditions and near normal\ntemperatures. For next weekend, models generally favor ridging\nacross the intermountain west. If this prevails, we'll see dry\nconditions continue with near normal to above normal temperatures.\nSome ECMWF ensemble members show a shortwave trough digging south\nalong the backside of the ridge and could graze Colorado. So there\nis a small chance (~10-20%) we could see cooler temperatures and\nlight precipitation if this system happens to pan out.",
